I. New in MACHXL v2.0
  A. Supported devices:
     In addition to the previously supported MACH355/435/445/465,
     MACHXL v2.0 supports
         JEDEC ON   MACH110/111/120/130/131/210/220/231
         JEDEC OFF  MACH211
     Of these, the MACHxx1 devices offer powerdown.
     See pages 393-394 of chapter 10 in the MACHXL v2.0
     Software User's Guide for a description of powerdown.

     The MACH111 device has two more clocks and significantly
     more routing resources than its pin-compatible predecessor,
     the MACH110.
     The MACH231 removes the "sibling block" routing restriction of
     its predecessor, the MACH230.

     For a tabular summary of MACH family and
     device features, see pages 377 through 379 of the device
     reference, chapter 10, in the MACHXL v2.0 Software User's Guide.
  B. Support of vector syntax has been enhanced
     in the simulation section. See pages 250 through 252 of
     the MACHXL v2.0 Software User's Guide.

     In the Simulation Section, all restrictions on the
     presence of GROUP names within the signal
     list of statements such as SETF have been removed.
     In other words, a signal list for a SETF, TRACE_ON,
     CLOCKF, PRELOAD, CHECK, or CHECKQ statement
     may contain group names and those group names
     may be defined in GROUP statements containing
     lists of signals AND some of those signals might be
     vectored signals ( i.e. signals with a 'range operator' ).
     See pages 150 and 151 of the MACHXL v2.0 Software User's Guide.

     Vectors may be viewed in the waveform display under
     VIEW:SIMULATION:TRACE SIGNALS ONLY:VECTORED.
     See pages 259 through 261 of the MACHXL v2.0 software
     user's guide.
  C. If device has XORs( 3xx/4xx ), then XNORs ( :*: )
     are converted by the minimizer to XORs instead of SOP.
     Subsequent XOR processing may retain the XOR expression
     or collapse XOR to SOP.
  D. An automatic node number conversion option for turning
     previously-fit and backannotated MACH110 design files
     into MACH111 design files has been added to the menu.
     See page 104 of the MACHXL v2.0 software user's guide.
  E. A "FLOAT NODES ONLY" option has been added to the menu.
     As a special case, this can be used to convert from MACH110
     to MACH111. More generally, it can be used to convert from
     MACHxx0 to MACHxx1. Still, more generally, it can be used
     to improve fitting on any design file with preplaced nodes.
     See page 104 of the MACHXL v2.0 software user's guide.
  F. Improved Fitting
      i) Iteration between partitioning and place/route.
           - a new time allotment ( see page 83 of the MACHXL v2.0
             Software User's Guide as modified by item VI. d. below
             in this readme file ), has been implemented
             for the up to 4 iterations;
           - an adaptive ( in view of prior iterations ) strategy
             is now used;
           - for 3xx/4xx, as a last resort, the partitioner
             switches to a more aggresive estimate of what
             will fit in the block. ( related to the XOR product term )
     ii) Hands-off partitioning for MACH1xx/2xx.
         Historically, at recommended utilization levels MACH1xx
         and MACH2xx have required manual partitioning via
         GROUP MACH_SEG_x statements ( where x denotes a block
         letter ). More often than not, with the MACHXL fitter,
         manual partitioning of MACH1xx/2xx devices is a hindrance
         rather than an assist to fitting.
     iii) New Fitting Options
         Spread Placement ( within blocks )
         Balanced Partitioning ( among blocks )
         See pages 84 & 85 of the MACHXL 2.0 Software User's Guide.
  G. New Syntax
     New syntax has been added to constrain individual signals to
     synchronous or asynchronous macrocell implementation in
     3xx/4xx devices:
        GROUP ASYNC_LIST  <async_list>
        GROUP SYNC_LIST   <sync_list>
     This syntax, described on pages 133 and 182 of the MACHXL
     v2.0 Software User's Guide, can be used to influence
     gate-splitting and to control the allocation of clocks
     emanating from global clock pins. In particular, a
     registered signal in <sync_list> cannot use a product
     term clock and, therefore, may be clocked without compromise
     in maximum operating frequency. For more information,
     see chapter 5 and pages 417 through 420 of
     chapter 10 of the MACHXL v2.0 Software User's Guide. 

     New syntax has been added to specify powerdown for
     individual signals ( xx1 devices )
        GROUP LOW_POWER_LIST <powered-down list>
     See pages 156 & 157 of the MACHXL v2.0 Software User's Guide.
  H. A JEDEC vector number, preceded by a V, has been added to
     the simulation output in the .LOG file for ease in cross
     referencing to the .JDC file. Also, the simulation command
     -- TRACE_ON, SETF, CLOCKF, PRELOAD, CHECK or CHECKQ --
     is displayed on the same line as the JEDEC vector number.
     Starting with the next line, is the relevant
     signal list with signals in the order of appearance in
     the corresponding JDC vector.
     Example:
        14-V6 - SETF
                /SET /RESET /CLKEN  /CLK  OE /IN

      This shows that the sixth test vector in the JDC file
      will have 6 signals in the indicated order and phase.
      Ignore the history file index number, in this example
      equal to 14, that is displayed prior to V6.

II. System configuration and installation
  1. See chapter 1 of the MACHXL Software User's Guide
     for detailed instructions on installing and
     configuring MACHXL software.

     Assuming your configuration is adequate, to install from
     the A drive:
        - place disk1 into drive A
        - from the DOS prompt, type
            A:install
        - wait for the MACHXL screen to appear and
        - press any key to proceed with the installation
          and respond to solicited choices.

     Installation from B drive is identical, with A in the above
     procedure replaced by B.

     MACHXL software is supported under DOS 5 and DOS 6 and
     as a DOS application under Windows and WINDOWS NT operating
     systems. See WIN31.DOC in the ..\MACHXL\DOC directory
     for a procedure to create a WINDOWS icon for MACHXL. Eight
     megabytes or more of extended memory is recommended.

     If you choose to keep one or more older versions of MACHXL
     on your system, be sure that the new version is installed
     in a separate directory. If, in addition, you choose to
     inhibit automatic update of autoexec.bat and config.sys,
     then you must perform the update manually BEFORE your first
     invocation of the new MACHXL version. Failure to do so, may
     corrupt several key files and thereby result in spurious
     operation of MACHXL.

  2. Parallel Port for programming JTAG devices via cable.
     The JTAG download cable cannot be used with some software keys.
     Remove all software keys from your PC's parallel port to which
     you connect the JTAG cable. Attach the other end
     to the 10-pin header on your JTAG circuit board (see the
     MACHPRO document ).

     ************************************************************
     ************************************************************
     IMPORTANT: To avoid damage to your computer, be sure that
     your COMPUTER IS POWERED ON BEFORE THE JTAG CIRCUIT BOARD
     is powered on.
     ************************************************************
     ************************************************************

  3. NT support notes for MACHXL 2.0
     MACHXL v2.0 is supported as a DOS application under
     WINDOWS NT.
     - If run in a DOS box under NT, MACHXL must be invoked as
       MACHXL -NT
     - MACHXL 2.0 has been tested under both
       Build528(NT3.1) and Build807 which is the
       RELEASE version (10/01/94) of NT 3.5.
       If you have Build511/512(NT 3.1), you should
       install ( the free ) patch USP2 to bring your
       system up to Build 528.
     - The DOS box in Windows NT has problems with
       both TSR's and DPMI services in general. Under
       rigorous use, you may find that you
       have occasional problems.
         +  If you are having lots of troubles,
            add "ntcmdprompt" to  %systemroot%\system32\config.nt.
            This is actually a NON-DOS compatibility option, but
            the software seems to work better with it than
            without.
         +  The DOS BOX may suddenly go into "print-screen"
            loop and will not stop  unless you go
            to the system menu for the DOS BOX and effect a
            CLOSE. This seems to be a DPMI-related problem
            which occurs more frequently on NT builds prior to 807.
         +  if your MACHXL software is frequently encountering
            General Protection Faults (GPFs), try modifying the
            WINNT\SYSTEM32\CONFIG.NT file. If there is no line
            containing "fcbs=", then add it. Else, modify the
            value which is probably either 4 or 16 and change
            it to 32. For example:
            fcbs=32,0
            The fcbs parameter controls the number of file
            control blocks available to an NT DOS session.
            It has been found to relieve some GPFs in
            applications other than MACHXL.
     -  Generally, performance is slightly better under NT than
        under DOS. However, under NT3.1 builds prior
        to 807, runtimes are occasionally 10x slower than under
        DOS due to a DPMI bug.

  4. MACHXL v2.0 software has not been tested in and is not
     supported in networked environments.  However, if you will be
     installing MACHXL in a networked environment, make certain that
     users run MACHXL software from a directory that they have
     read/write access to.

  5. MACHXL v2.0 software is not supported under DR-DOS.
     Limited testing demonstrates that MACHXL may be run as a
     DOS application under OS-2. However, AMD cannot guarantee
     its operation in that environment.

III. Retargeting 1xx/2xx designs to MACH3xx/4xx
    When retargeting a MACH1xx/2xx design to a MACH3xx/4xx device,
    it may be necessary to modify certain equations. This is
    a consequence of supporting MACH1xx/2xx in a manner
    compatible with old designs which were fit under PALASM.

    EXAMPLE 1:
    In a 1xx/2xx design, the implied or unnamed default clock will
    be identified with a named clock placed on the default clock
    pin.  However, the identification is avoided and an error
    results in this circumstance for MACH 3xx/4xx. The solution
    in this case is to either float the named clock, preplace it
    at another location, or to remove the implied default clock
    from the design by replacing it with the named clock.

    EXAMPLE 2:
    In a MACH1xx/2xx design, the fitter will issue a warning when
    a signal is defined non-ground and identical SET and RESET.
    This may happen inadvertently due to the set/reset banking
    of MACH1xx/2xx! In MACH3xx/4xx, it spurs an error which
    terminates the fitting process.

IV. Known problems with MACHXL version 2.0
    1. False Warning
       In certain instances, the Boolean Postprocess  (MACHBPP),
       will issue a warning
          |> WARNING B626
                The number in the numeric equation is not
                large enough to represent the signal label(s)
                on the lefthand side. The higher order signal
                values will be assigned the value ZERO.

      for an IF construct where a comparison is made between a binary
      number and a group/vector of pins/nodes. This warning is
      unnecessary and misleading in the instance where the binary
      number being compared STARTS (left-hand-side) with a leading
      0 (i.e. #b01111111). The leading 0 is getting stripped
      internally and when the check for proper bit-matching is made,
      there are LESS bits remaining (in this case 7 instead of 8)
      and hence the warning tells the user that the number of bits
      on the LHS and RHS do not match while the SOURCE file shows
      they do indeed match. In this situation, the warning can be
      ignored.
   2. Out of memory messages
      If you encounter an out of memory message, check to see that you
      have the recommended minimum memory configuration of 8 MB.

      Other situations that might occur:
      a. When the parser is invoked as a DOS application under
         Windows, Windows NT or OS2 and the current design has a
         large number of very complex CASE statements, the
         Parser may run out of memory.  If this happens, try
         running MACHXL directly from DOS.
      b. If the Minimizer reports an out of memory condition, try
         rerunning the design using fast minimization (found in the Logic
         Synthesis menu). This problem usually occurs when the logic
         synthesis options "Optimize Registers for D/T-type" is set to
         "Change all to D-type" or "Change  all to T-type",
         "Use Automatic Gate Splitting" is set to "Y", and "Ensure
         polarity after minimization is" is set to "Best for device".
         The solution is to reduce the number of minimization options
         selected. "Ensure polarity after minimization is: Best for
         device" is the most memory intensive of these options.
      c. If the Minimizer reports a 64K segment limit condition, it has
         encountered an equation that has a large number of product
         terms (i.e. 25 to 30) where each product term has only one
         or two unique signal names. A sparse Karnaugh map results,
         which is unlikely to reduce further. To correct the
         problem, try turning on fast minimization or manually gate
         splitting the offending equation.

V. Software Trouble Reporting
    To report a MACHXL software problem such as a general protection
    fault or internal error, refer to the file \MACHXL\README.BUG or
    to Appendix D of the MACHXL revision 2.0 Software User's Guide.

VI. Errors or Omissions in the MACHXL v2.0 Software User's Guide
      a. SIGNATURE
         On page 175, the beginning of the second paragraph under
         REMARKS should read

         In a number signature, the signature form is <radix><number> ...
         where the second reference to signature should be italicized.
      b. SIGNATURE
         On page 176, the implied assertion that the U field
         will be represented in the JEDEC as ASCII or HEX
         ( i.e. with a UA or UH prefix ) is false. Although
         ASCII and HEX signatures are allowed as input, they
         will be represented in the JEDEC and after disassembly as
         BINARY.
      c. The footnote at the bottom of page 417, should also
         mention that the letter designators,
         'A' for asynchronous and 'S' for synchronous, may be found
         in the 3xx/4xx .RPT file in the "type" column of a
         "BLOCK X LOGIC MACROCELLS" section wherein the signal
         was placed. Therefore, it is not mandatory that the
         user consult the .PRD file to determine if a signal
         was implemented async or sync.
      d. Time allocation for MODE 1 and MODE 2 on page 83
         should read as follows:

          MODE 1
          The "Iterate between partition & place/route" option
          is set to "Y" and the Run Time Upper Bound in 15 minutes"
          option is set to a value L greater than 0.

          Time remaining = 15 * L - ( the time elapsed since
          entering the first iteration of partitioning ). Time
          remaining is reported by place and route. Time remaining
          is checked several times within each iteration of
          PARTITIONING. If time has runs out ( i.e. time remaining
          is less than or equal to zero ), then terminate with
          non-success.
          If time does not run out in a successful
          partitioning iteration, then place/route is called with
          an iteration time limit of (L*15)/4 minutes.
          Time remaining is reported by place/route relative
          to the place/route iteration time limit.

          MODE 2
          The "Iterate between partition & place/route" option
          is set to "Y" and the Run Time Upper Bound in 15 minutes"
          option is set to 0. No time limit is imposed on the
          Partitioner. A time limit of four hours is imposed on
          each of the first three place/route iterations. No time
          limit is imposed on the fourth and final place/route
          iteration.

